/*
  there are 3 timer in the ATmega328P: timer0 timer1 and timer2. timer1 is used by the Servo lib
  so we wont use that timer even though its the only 16bit timer the rest are 8bit timers.
  timer0 is for millis() delay() micros() delayMicroseconds()
  therefore we will use timer 0 for ourer millis() delay() and so on.
  timer2 is for things like for example tone() so it doesnt block the whole programm after
  calling it. in this little explanation file i explain the registers you need
  so you can know whats under the hood and how everything works.
  .
  first of all we need to know some registers and what they do
  .
  first there are the prescaler
  what do are they for? - well they do one main point as you may know the Arduino Unos clock speed is
  16 Mhz and thats just too fast for most things like leds servos motores and so on but you can slow
  it down if you use a presacler here are the diffrent prescaler: (us is micro seconds just so you know)
  .
  prescaler   |   clock-speeds   |  time to countstep   |   overflow-freqency (8bit)
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  .  1        |    16     MHz    |      0.0625 us       |           62.5   kHz
  .  8        |    2      MHz    |      0.5    us       |           7.81   kHz
  .  32       |    500    khz    |      2      us       |           1.95   kHz
  .  64       |    250    kHz    |      4      us       |           976.5  Hz
  .  128      |    125    khz    |      8      us       |           488.3  Hz
  .  256      |    62.5   kHz    |      16     us       |           244.1  Hz
  .  1024     |    15.625 kHz    |      64     us       |           61.0   Hz
*/
/*
  okay now you know the prescaler and what they do but how do you activate them?
  there is a makro for that called Clock Select(CS) but there is a catch timer0 and timer1
  combinations are diffrent to timer2 combinations so i will give you two tabels one for timer 0/1 and one
  for timer 2
  .
  timer 2:
  . CS22 |  CS21 |  CS20 | precaler | aditional info
  ----------------------------------|----------------
  .  0   |   0   |   0   |    -     |  TIMER-STOPS
  .  0   |   0   |   1   |    1     |     normal
  .  0   |   1   |   0   |    8     |     normal
  .  0   |   1   |   1   |    32    |     normal
  .  1   |   0   |   0   |    64    |     normal
  .  1   |   0   |   1   |   128    |     normal
  .  1   |   1   |   0   |   256    |     normal
  .  1   |   1   |   1   |   1024   |     normal
  .
  timer 0/timer 1 (the 0 behind CS has to change to 1 if it is used for timer 1)
  . CS02 |  CS01 |  CS00 | precaler |             aditional info
  ----------------------------------|--------------------------------------
  .  0   |   0   |   0   |    -     |              TIMER-STOPS
  .  0   |   0   |   1   |    1     |                normal
  .  0   |   1   |   0   |    8     |                normal
  .  0   |   1   |   1   |    64    |                normal
  .  1   |   0   |   0   |   256    |                normal
  .  1   |   0   |   1   |   1024   |                normal
  .  1   |   1   |   0   |    -     |  extern clock on pin TO/T1 FALLING
  .  1   |   1   |   1   |    -     |  extern clock on pin TO/T1 RISING
  .
  as you have noticed this CSxbit is not a register but a makro for bits now we come to the actual
  register TCCRxB where you activate the prescalers thats about it here is a example for a prescaler of 64 on timer 0
*/
void example()
{
    TCCR0B = 0;                          // all bits reset so we can controll them our self
    TCCR0B |= (1 << CS00) | (1 << CS01); // bits 0 and 1 set to 1
}
/*
  TCCRxA is the other register we need to select the mode the timer has to run on here is another tabel
  (im sorry i know this seems like much but remeber you dont need to memorize these you can look it up for example in this file
  i dont know these out of my head either i too look it up and write it down(this file))
  .
  timer 0 (8bit) and timer 2 (8bit):
   WGMx2 (TCCRxB) | WGMx1 (TCCRxA) | WGMx0(TCCRxA) |           mode            | highest-count | describtion
  ----------------|----------------|---------------|---------------------------|---------------|---------------------------------------
  .       0       |        0       |       0       | normal                    |      255      | standard counter 0 - 255
  .       0       |        0       |       1       | PWM, phase correct        |      255      | counts up and down(symetric impulses)
  .       0       |        1       |       0       | ctc                       |     OCRxA     | counts to the limit set by OCRxA then jumps to 0
  .       0       |        1       |       1       | fast PWM (8bit)           |      255      | standard 8bit PWM
  .       1       |        0       |       0       | reserved                  |       -       | do not use
  .       1       |        0       |       1       | PWM, phase & freq correct |     OCRxA     | freqeny can be controlled by OCRxA
  .       1       |        1       |       0       | reserved                  |       -       | do not use
  .       1       |        1       |       1       | fast PWM (var)            |     OCRxA     | freqeny can be controlled by OCRxA
  .
  timer 1 (16bit):
   WGM13 (TCCR1B) | WGM12 (TCCR1B) | WGM11 (TCCR1A) | WGM10(TCCR1A) |            mode            | highest-count
  ----------------|----------------|----------------|---------------|----------------------------|---------------
  .       0       |        0       |        0       |       0       | normal                     |     65535
  .       0       |        0       |        0       |       1       | PWM, phase correct (8bit)  |     255
  .       0       |        0       |        1       |       0       | PWM, phase correct (9bit)  |     511
  .       0       |        0       |        1       |       1       | PWM, phase correct (10bit) |     1023
  .       0       |        1       |        0       |       0       | ctc                        |     OCR1A
  .       0       |        1       |        0       |       1       | fast PWM (8bit)            |     255
  .       0       |        1       |        1       |       0       | fast PWM (9bit)            |     511
  .       0       |        1       |        1       |       1       | fast PWM (10bit)           |     1023
  .       1       |        0       |        0       |       0       | PWM, phase & freq correct  |     ICR1
  .       1       |        0       |        0       |       1       | PWM, phase & freq correct  |     OCR1A
  .       1       |        0       |        1       |       0       | PWM, phase correct         |     ICR1
  .       1       |        0       |        1       |       1       | PWM, phase correct         |     OCR1A
  .       1       |        1       |        0       |       0       | ctc                        |     ICR1
  .       1       |        1       |        0       |       1       | reserved                   |       -
  .       1       |        1       |        1       |       0       | fast PWM (var)             |     ICR1
  .       1       |        1       |        1       |       1       | fast PWM (var)             |     OCR1A
  .
  alright this took longer than expected but now that thats out of the way we can finaly activate an interrupt
  with TIMSKx here i again made a tabel because its the easierst way to explain a little bit below i also will
  give you an example so you can know how to use it
  .
  bits in TIMSKx           | when does an interrupt fire?            | ISR makro
  -------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-------------------
  TOIE0 / TOIE1 / TOIE2    | when counter overflows 255 -> 0 (8bit)  | TIMERx_OVF_vect
  -------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-------------------
  OCIE0A / OCIE1A / OCIE2A | when counter TCNTx is the same as OCRxA | TIMERx_COMPA_vect
  -------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-------------------
  OCIE0B / OCIE1B / OCIE2B | when counter TCNTx is the same as OCRxB | TIMERx_COMPB_vect
  -------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-------------------
  ICIE1 (only timer 1)     | when a signal edge is detected on the   | TIMER1_CAPT_vect
  .                        | ICP1 pin (pin8) or by the anlog         |
  .                        | comparator, the counter value (TCNT1)   |
  .                        | is immediately captured in ICR1.        |
  .
  here a lil example how it works:
*/
void example()
{
    TCCR0A |= (1 << WGM01) | (1 << WGM00); // select a mode in this case it is fast PWM
    TCCR0B |= (1 << CS01) | (1 << CS00);   // prescaler for normal things just use 64

    TIMSK0 |= (1 << TOIE0); // when iterrupt should fire
}
ISR(TIMER0_OVF_vect) // vector for TOIE0
{
    Serial.print("this does something");
    /* CODE */
}

